                <h1>SOP: Setting Up Ad Account Low Balance Alerts to Prevent Campaign Disruption</h1>
<h2>Why Low Balance Alerts Are Critical During Scaling</h2>
<p>The biggest threat during a campaign scaling phase isn't ad creative fatigue—it's running out of account funds mid-flight. When your budget stops spending, the traffic window closes and competitors move in. Many operations teams have experienced this scenario: checking accounts Sunday morning only to find the balance hit zero at 3 AM, losing three hours of prime conversion traffic. The more insidious problem is that some platforms automatically downgrade bidding strategies when funds are low, causing CPA to spike without immediate awareness.</p>
<p>Setting up low balance alerts isn't optional—it's fundamental to protecting your scaling results. This SOP applies to teams managing 3 or more ad accounts.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Understand Each Platform's Alert Mechanism</h2>
<p>Different platforms have different billing logics and alert configuration locations. Start by mapping out each platform's baseline rules.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Platform</th>
<th>Billing Model</th>
<th>Alert Location</th>
<th>Notification Methods</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Meta Business</td>
<td>Spend-based</td>
<td>BM &gt; Payments &gt; Low Balance Alert</td>
<td>Email + System Notification</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google Ads</td>
<td>Credit-based</td>
<td>Tools &gt; Billing &gt; Triggers</td>
<td>Email</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok Business</td>
<td>Prepaid</td>
<td>Account Overview &gt; Alert Settings</td>
<td>Email + SMS</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Third-party (Apple Search Ads)</td>
<td>Spend-based</td>
<td>Settings &gt; Account Preferences</td>
<td>Email only</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Ops Tip</strong>: If your team manages multiple agency or sub-accounts, build a separate expense ledger in your financial reconciliation system rather than relying solely on platform notifications—platform alerts can have 2-4 hour delays.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 2: Configure Two-Tier Alert Thresholds</h2>
<p>One generic "low balance" warning isn't enough. The scientific approach is two threshold levels:</p>
<h3>Tier 1: Early Warning (Balance = Estimated Daily Spend × 2)</h3>
<p>Trigger: Account balance covers approximately two days of normal spending.</p>
<p>Purpose: Give finance and ops time to prepare recharge without urgent payment requests.</p>
<p>Action: Ops confirms recharge application submitted within 4 hours.</p>
<h3>Tier 2: Urgent Alert (Balance = Estimated Daily Spend × 0.5)</h3>
<p>Trigger: Account balance only covers half a day of spending.</p>
<p>Purpose: Immediate recharge action to avoid impacting evening peak traffic competition.</p>
<p>Action: Finance processes this account's recharge with priority; ops monitors account status simultaneously.</p>
<p><strong>How to calculate "estimated daily spend"?</strong> Use the 7-day average of account spend, not single-day peaks. If the account is in rapid scaling mode with increasing daily spend, multiply thresholds by a 1.2 safety factor.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Set Notification Delivery Rules</h2>
<p>Alerts are useless if nobody sees them. This is the most common failure point in multi-account management.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended notification assignment:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Account Owner (Ops)</strong>: Receives both alert tiers to ensure response.</li>
<li><strong>Finance Lead</strong>: Receives only Tier 1 alerts as recharge approval triggers.</li>
<li><strong>Team Lead (Optional)</strong>: Receives only Tier 2 urgent alerts for major risk awareness.</li>
</ol>
<p>Notification channel priority: SMS &gt; Instant messaging (DingTalk/Feishu) &gt; Email. Email serves as archive and shouldn't be the sole channel.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Configure Auto-Recharge (Advanced)</h2>
<p>If your accounts have high daily spend exceeding $5,000, enable platform auto-recharge features:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meta</strong>: In payment settings, link a backup credit card and set "Auto-reload $X when balance falls below $Y."</li>
<li><strong>Google Ads</strong>: Enable "Auto-recharge" with specified threshold and spending cap.</li>
<li><strong>TikTok</strong>: Supports "Auto-deduct when balance insufficient" mode with minimum balance line and reload amount settings.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: Auto-recharge requires finance pre-approval of budget limits and monthly review of actual auto-recharge spending against plan.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 5: Establish Daily Inspection Routine</h2>
<p>Even with alert systems in place, ops teams need daily routine checks. Recommended inspection schedule:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>9:00 AM</strong>: Ops opening check of all account balance status, confirming no anomalies.</li>
<li><strong>2:00 PM</strong>: Second round for high-spend accounts, especially before afternoon traffic peaks.</li>
<li><strong>9:00 PM</strong>: End-of-day check to prevent overnight depletion causing next-day restart delays.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each inspection takes only 5-10 minutes but effectively prevents hidden interruptions outside working hours.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Emergency Response After Disruption</h2>
<p>Despite all preparations, alerts may be missed or recharge delayed. When discovering a paused account, follow these steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Confirm interruption duration</strong>: Check account spend records to determine the gap between balance depletion and discovery.</li>
<li><strong>Assess damage scope</strong>: Identify affected specific ad groups and campaigns, estimate missed impressions and conversion opportunities.</li>
<li><strong>Immediate recharge</strong>: Prioritize the account with lowest balance; monitor recovery status after recharge.</li>
<li><strong>Notify stakeholders</strong>: Inform投放负责人 and clients (if involving outsourced accounts) about interruption status and expected recovery time.</li>
<li><strong>Root cause review</strong>: Record direct cause of this interruption (missed alert? failed recharge? sudden spend spike?), update SOP to close gaps.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Quick Checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Platform alert thresholds set at 0.5× and 2× daily spend</li>
<li>[ ] SMS and instant messaging notification channels tested and working</li>
<li>[ ] Notification list covers ops, finance, and leadership tiers</li>
<li>[ ] Auto-recharge enabled (if applicable) with budget caps set</li>
<li>[ ] Three-times-daily inspection added to workflow</li>
<li>[ ] Team members briefed on interruption emergency SOP</li>
</ul>
<p>Following this SOP will reduce balance-depletion-driven campaign disruptions by over 90%. Balance management is the infrastructure of overseas advertising—don't let it become your growth bottleneck.</p>
